.TH LAYERFS 4
.SH NAME
layerfs \- a directory where any file written is a layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount -ac {layerfs}
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Layerfs
serves an empty directory in which any file may be created, read,
written and removed.
Union-mounted with create
.RB ( -c )
over a video feed's
.B layer
directory, it makes whatever is written into the stack a layer
.RI ( layers (2)):
a file written once, or one written to continuously, a stream.
.PP
A file keeps only what is due now: its records for the newest frame
written, and its
.B frame=*
records, never the frames before.
A line that is no record is not kept.
A read is not at an offset: an open's first read gives what is due, in
one read, and each read after gives the records written since, waiting
while there are none.
So a viewer reads a file written once as it reads a vision session's
detections.
.PP
Opening a file to write with truncation (the shell's
.BR > )
rewrites it: what it held is gone, and its version
.RB ( qid.vers )
and modification time change, which is how
.IR layers (2)
knows to read it again.
Writing on
.RB ( >> ,
or a writer that stays open) changes neither.
A file removed ends its readers.
.SH EXAMPLES
A region drawn on every frame of the camera, then only people boxed,
live, then a head count, then the count taken off:
.IP
.EX
mount -ac {layerfs} /mnt/video/0/layer
echo 'frame=* box x0=100 y0=80 x1=500 y1=400 label=door' > /mnt/video/0/layer/roi
cat /mnt/vision/1/detections | grep 'class=person' > /mnt/video/0/layer/people &
rm /mnt/video/0/layer/people
.EE
.PP
The shell takes
.B =
in a word as an assignment, so a pattern with one is quoted.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/layerfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR layers (2),
.IR visionfs (4),
.IR wm-camera (1)
